course: dictionary
lesson: 6808
title: Words from Japan: origami
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: «Origami is the Japanese-rooted art of folding paper into shapes without cutting or gluing.» Origami is a noun, the name of an art. The clues tell you what it is: it uses paper, it makes shapes, and no scissors or glue are needed.
explain: The sentence also says «sharp creases and corners lined up exactly make the model neat.» A crease is the line left when you fold paper. Lining up corners exactly means the edges meet with no gaps, so the model looks neat.
explain: The ladybird example uses origami to describe something that is not paper: «thin wings unfold from underneath like origami.» Like origami means the wings open out in neat folds, the way paper does. Context helps you picture the meaning even when the word is used in a new way.
fact: Origami means the art of folding paper into shapes, usually without cutting or glue.
fact: Origami comes from Japan, and it is a Japanese-rooted art.
fact: «Crease» means a line made by folding paper.
fact: The famous origami model is the paper crane, a bird.
fact: In Japan, folding 1000 paper cranes is linked to wishes and peace.
exercise: «Origami is the Japanese-rooted art of folding paper into shapes without cutting or gluing.» What is origami? | A kind of Japanese paper lamp | A glue that sticks paper together | *The art of folding paper into shapes | A way of cutting paper with scissors
exercise: In «sharp creases and corners lined up exactly make the model neat», which word means the line made by folding? | neat | *creases | corners | model
exercise: Maya folds a paper frog without any scissors or glue. Is this origami? | No, origami only makes boxes | No, origami always needs scissors | No, a frog is not a shape | *Yes, origami uses folding, so no scissors or glue are needed
test: «A beetle's thin back wings fold like origami to fit underneath.» What does fold like origami mean here? | Cut into small pieces | *Fold neatly, in careful folds like paper | Fall off and float away | Stuck down with glue
test: Which of these is a famous origami model? | A wooden table | A metal spoon | A glass of water | *A paper crane
exam: Which is the best meaning of origami? | *The Japanese art of folding paper into shapes, usually without cutting or glue | A way to cut paper into stars | A type of bird | A kind of paper bag
